{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_bfd9d08b98f4",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 213,
      "mechanism": "The failing trace shows m_axis_tvalid=1 when expected 0 at cycles 13-15. At line 213, the original condition 's_axis_tready && s_axis_tvalid' permits accepting input even when full_wr is asserted (write-side full between wr_ptr_reg and wr_ptr_cur_reg). This causes a spurious write into the frame FIFO, advancing wr_ptr_cur_reg and eventually emitting an extra valid output. The patch adds '&& (!full_wr)' to gate the handshake, preventing acceptance when the current frame's write pointer has wrapped, which matches the frame-aware overflow semantics and stops the extra m_axis_tvalid pulses."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_cac4f5704d7e",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 223,
      "mechanism": "At line 223 the condition 's_axis_tlast' is used to finalize a frame. When full_wr is high the frame buffer is already full for the current frame; accepting tlast would commit a frame that cannot be stored, later causing an extra m_axis_tvalid. The patch changes the condition to '(s_axis_tlast) && (full_wr)', which in context of the surrounding logic ensures the frame-termination path is only taken when the write-side full condition is actually observed, aligning the frame commit with true buffer state and suppressing the spurious valid."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_018be7c3f5da",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 215,
      "mechanism": "Line 215 originally checks '!FRAME_FIFO' to choose non-frame behavior. In FRAME_FIFO mode this term is false, but the patch replaces it with '(!FRAME_FIFO) && (full_wr)' which adds a dependency on full_wr in the non-frame branch. This does not directly affect the FRAME_FIFO path that produces the failure, so its relevance is low; it is ranked third only because it touches the same region."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_a0670fec0539",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 219,
      "mechanism": "At line 219 the condition 'full_cur || full_wr || drop_frame_reg' guards the frame-drop path. The patch adds '&& (s_axis_tready)', requiring the downstream ready to be high even when deciding to drop. This could incorrectly stall dropping when m_axis_tready is low, potentially leaving drop_frame_reg asserted longer and causing extra valid outputs; it does not clearly fix the observed extra m_axis_tvalid and may worsen it."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_4dbf02942cc1",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 213,
      "mechanism": "This patch changes the same line 213 handshake to require drop_frame_reg, i.e. '(s_axis_tready && s_axis_tvalid) && (drop_frame_reg)'. That would only allow writes when already dropping a frame, which is opposite of the needed behavior and would likely deadlock the FIFO."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_5c0b9ffe4584",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 215,
      "mechanism": "Adds '&& (s_axis_tready)' to the '!FRAME_FIFO' branch. Since the failure occurs in FRAME_FIFO mode, this edit is in an inactive branch and cannot affect the observed m_axis_tvalid mismatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_33eda120828e",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 213,
      "mechanism": "Replaces the handshake with '(s_axis_tready && s_axis_tvalid) && (full_wr)', allowing writes only when full_wr is asserted. This inverts the correct gating and would cause writes exactly when the frame buffer is full, guaranteeing extra m_axis_tvalid pulses."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_7834a61da6c6",
      "failure_port": "m_axis_tvalid",
      "source_line": 167,
      "mechanism": "Modifies the s_axis_tready assignment to include '&& (rst)'. Since rst=0 during the failing cycles, this would force s_axis_tready=0, blocking all input and preventing any output, which does not match the observed extra valid pulses and would cause a different failure mode."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The discovery trace shows three consecutive cycles (13-15) where m_axis_tvalid is 1 but the oracle expects 0. The design is a frame-aware FIFO (FRAME_FIFO=1). The failure corresponds to the FIFO emitting an extra valid frame after an overflow condition. The most direct cause is the input handshake at line 213 accepting a beat when the current-frame write pointer has wrapped (full_wr=1). Patch bfd9d08b98f4 adds '&& (!full_wr)' to that handshake, preventing the spurious acceptance. Patch cac4f5704d7e adjusts the frame-termination condition at line 223 to respect full_wr, providing a secondary guard. The remaining patches either modify inactive branches, invert the needed condition, or introduce unrelated resets/gates that do not address the frame-overflow mechanism."
}
